    39	
    40	## Statement
    41	
    42	**False claim:** every countably compact topological space
    43	([[def-compactness-variants]]) is compact ([[def-compact-space]]).
    44	
    45	**Where the claim comes from, and what is actually true.** Countable compactness
    46	tests only the at most countable open covers, and the claim above asserts that
    47	testing those is enough. It is enough when the space is also Lindel&ouml;f, and
    48	the claim above drops that hypothesis.
    49	
    50	**The refutation assumes the Axiom of Countable Choice**
    51	([[def-countable-choice]]), which is what makes each witness countably compact.
    52	Two witnesses are available and both are refutations on their own: the first
    53	uncountable ordinal $\omega_1$ with its order topology
    54	([[def-first-uncountable-ordinal]], [[lem-the-order-topology-on-an-ordinal]]), and
    55	the closed long ray ([[def-the-long-line]]).
    56	
    57	## Facts & Assumptions
    58	
    59	**Given:** The first uncountable ordinal $\omega_1$ with its order topology, the closed long ray $R$ with its order topology, and the Axiom of Countable Choice.
    60	
    61	[A1] The false claim: every countably compact topological space is compact.
    62	
    63	[A2] The Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]).
    64	
    65	[L1] Assuming countable choice, $\omega_1$ with its order topology is countably compact and is not compact ([[thm-ordinal-spaces-and-compactness]], claim 3).
    66	
    67	[L2] Assuming countable choice, the closed long ray $R$ is countably compact, and it is not compact and not Lindel&ouml;f ([[thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact]], claims 2, 3 and 4; [[def-the-long-line]]).
    68	
    69	## Refutation
    70	
    71	**Proof technique:** contradiction.
    72	
    73	1.1 Suppose the claim [A1] holds, so that every countably compact space is compact. [A1, assume-contra]
    74	
    75	1.2 Assuming [A2], the space $\omega_1$ with its order topology is countably compact by [L1], and the closed long ray $R$ is countably compact by [L2]. [A2, L1, L2]
    76	
    77	2.1 By [A1] and step 1.2 both $\omega_1$ and $R$ would be compact. [A1, step 1.2]
    78	
    79	2.2 Neither is: $\omega_1$ is not compact by [L1], and $R$ is not compact by [L2]. [L1, L2, step 1.2]
    80	
    81	3.1 Steps 2.1 and 2.2 contradict each other, so the claim [A1] is false, and each of the two spaces refutes it on its own. [A1, step 2.1, step 2.2, discharge-contradiction] ∎
    82	
    83	## Remarks
    84	
    85	**Why the missing hypothesis is Lindel&ouml;fness and not something weaker.** Countable compactness plus Lindel&ouml;fness does give compactness ([[thm-compactness-variants-hierarchy]], claim 1(b)), so a countably compact non-compact space must fail to be Lindel&ouml;f. The long ray is checked to fail it directly ([[thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact]], claim 4), and $\omega_1$ fails it for the same reason: the cover by initial segments has no at most countable subcover, an at most countable set of countable ordinals being bounded.
    86	
    87	**The two witnesses are not the same space and neither is redundant.** The ordinal $\omega_1$ is also sequentially compact, so it separates compactness from sequential compactness as well ([[fs-sequentially-compact-implies-compact]]); the long ray is a linear continuum and is connected, so it also shows that connectedness contributes nothing to compactness.
