FILE items/def-sequence.md
4:title: "Sequences of reals: bounded, eventually, frequently, tails, subsequences"
47:## Definition
48:
49:Throughout, $\mathbb{R}$ is the complete ordered field ([[def-complete-ordered-field]],
50:[[def-real-numbers]]) with its order and absolute value ([[def-real-order]]), and
51:$\mathbb{N}$ is the set of natural numbers with its order ([[def-natural-numbers]],
52:[[def-nat-order]]).
53:
54:A **sequence of reals** is a function $x : \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{R}$. We write
55:$x_k$ for the value $x(k)$, call it the **$k$-th term**, and write $(x_k)$, or
56:$(x_k)_{k \in \mathbb{N}}$, for the function itself. The **range** of $(x_k)$ is
57:the subset $\{\, x_k : k \in \mathbb{N} \,\} \subseteq \mathbb{R}$.
58:
59:Let $(x_k)$ be a sequence of reals and let $P$ be a property of indices.
60:
61:- $(x_k)$ is **bounded** if there is $M \in \mathbb{R}$ with $|x_k| \le M$ for
62:  every $k \in \mathbb{N}$; it is **unbounded** otherwise.
63:- $P$ holds **eventually** if there is $K \in \mathbb{N}$ such that $P(k)$ holds
64:  for every $k \ge K$.
65:- $P$ holds **frequently** if for every $K \in \mathbb{N}$ there is some
66:  $k \ge K$ for which $P(k)$ holds.
67:- For $K \in \mathbb{N}$, the **$K$-th tail** of $(x_k)$ is the sequence
68:  $x^{(K)}$ defined by $x^{(K)}_j := x_{j + K}$ for $j \in \mathbb{N}$; it is
69:  again a sequence of reals.
70:- A function $n : \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}$ is **strictly increasing** if
71:  $n_j < n_k$ whenever $j < k$. For such an $n$, the **subsequence** of $(x_k)$
72:  along $n$ is the composite $x \circ n$, written $(x_{n_k})_{k \in \mathbb{N}}$;
73:  it is again a sequence of reals.
74:
75:**Convergence and Cauchyness are not defined here.** They are already fixed, for
76:sequences of reals, by the published [[def-real-limit]]: $(x_k)$ *converges* to
77:$x \in \mathbb{R}$ when for every rational $\varepsilon > 0$ there is
78:$K \in \mathbb{N}$ with $|x_k - x| < \varepsilon$ for all $k \ge K$, and $(x_k)$
79:is *Cauchy* when for every rational $\varepsilon > 0$ there is $K \in \mathbb{N}$
80:with $|x_k - x_l| < \varepsilon$ for all $k, l \ge K$. This page builds the
81:toolkit for those two notions and does not restate them. A sequence
82:**converges** if it converges to some real, and **diverges** if it does not.
83:
FILE items/lem-index-map-grows.md
4:title: "A strictly increasing index map satisfies $n_k \\ge k$"
44:## Statement
45:
46:Let $n : \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}$ be a function, written $k \mapsto n_k$, and
47:recall that $n$ is **strictly increasing** when $n_j < n_k$ whenever $j < k$
48:([[def-sequence]], [[def-nat-order]]).
49:
50:1. **Consecutive comparisons suffice.** If $n_i < n_{\sigma(i)}$ for every
51:   $i \in \mathbb{N}$, then $n$ is strictly increasing.
52:2. **Growth.** If $n$ is strictly increasing then $n_k \ge k$ for every
53:   $k \in \mathbb{N}$.
54:
55:Claim 1 is what one checks in practice when exhibiting a subsequence; claim 2 is
56:what every later subsequence argument uses.
57:
FILE items/def-compactness-variants.md
4:title: "Countably compact, Lindel\\\"of, sequentially compact, limit point compact and $\\sigma$-compact spaces, and relatively compact subsets"
31:## Definition
32:
33:Let $(X, \mathcal{T})$ be a topological space ([[def-topological-space]]), with
34:open covers, subcovers, finiteness and compactness as in [[def-compact-space]],
35:and *finite*, *at most countable* and *uncountable* as in [[def-countable]].
36:
37:- $(X, \mathcal{T})$ is **countably compact** when every open cover of $X$ that
38:  is at most countable has a finite subcover.
39:- $(X, \mathcal{T})$ is **Lindel&ouml;f** when every open cover of $X$ has an at
40:  most countable subcover.
41:- $(X, \mathcal{T})$ is **sequentially compact** when every sequence $(x_k)$ in
42:  $X$, that is every function $x : \mathbb{N} \to X$
43:  ([[def-sequence-convergence-top]]), has a subsequence $(x_{n_j})$ converging to
44:  a point of $X$, the index map $n : \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}$ being strictly
45:  increasing ([[def-sequence]], [[lem-index-map-grows]]).
46:- $(X, \mathcal{T})$ is **limit point compact** when every infinite subset
47:  $A \subseteq X$ has a limit point in $X$, that is a point $p \in X$ every
48:  neighbourhood $N$ of which satisfies $N \cap (A \setminus \{p\}) \ne \varnothing$
49:  ([[def-interior-closure-boundary-top]]). Here *infinite* means not finite in
50:  the sense of [[def-countable]].
51:- $(X, \mathcal{T})$ is **$\sigma$-compact** when there is an at most countable
52:  family $\mathcal{K}$ of compact subsets of $X$ with $X = \bigcup \mathcal{K}$.
53:- A subset $A \subseteq X$ is **relatively compact in $X$** when its closure
54:  $\overline{A}$ ([[def-interior-closure-boundary-top]]) is a compact subset of
55:  $X$.
56:
57:A subset $A \subseteq X$ is called countably compact, Lindel&ouml;f,
58:sequentially compact, limit point compact or $\sigma$-compact when the subspace
59:$(A, \mathcal{T}_A)$ is ([[def-subspace-topology-top]]), exactly as for
60:compactness. **Relative compactness is the exception and is deliberately not of
61:that form**: it is a statement about $A$ *inside* $X$, since $\overline{A}$ is
62:computed in $X$, and a set may be relatively compact in one space and not in
63:another that contains it. Every other notion on this list is intrinsic to the
64:subspace.
65:
66:**The countable covers may be listed.** A nonempty at most countable family
67:$\mathcal{U}$ admits a surjection $\mathbb{N} \to \mathcal{U}$
68:([[lem-countable-iff-surjection-from-n]]), so countable compactness says: for
69:every sequence $(U_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$ of open sets with
70:$X = \bigcup_{n \in \mathbb{N}} U_n$ there are finitely many indices whose sets
71:already cover $X$. That surjection is produced from the countability assumption
72:alone and no choice principle is involved; the empty family covers only the empty
73:space, which is compact anyway.
74:
75:**Indexing starts at $0$.** A sequence here is a function on $\mathbb{N}$ and
76:$\mathbb{N}$ contains $0$ ([[def-sequence]]), so a subsequence is
77:$(x_{n_j})_{j \in \mathbb{N}}$ with $n_0 < n_1 < \cdots$ and $n_j \ge j$
78:([[lem-index-map-grows]]). An index range taken from a text that starts at $1$
79:must be shifted before it is used here.
80:
81:**Agreement with the metric definitions.** Let $(X,d)$ be a metric space carrying
82:its metric topology $\mathcal{T}_d$ ([[def-metric-topology]],
83:[[def-metrizable-space]]). Then the three notions that
84:[[def-metric-compactness-variants]] defines metrically are the three defined
85:above, read in $(X, \mathcal{T}_d)$:
86:
87:- *Countably compact.* The open sets used there are the members of
88:  $\mathcal{T}_d$, so the at most countable open covers are the same families and
89:  the condition is the same condition, exactly as for compactness itself
90:  ([[thm-compactness-agrees-with-metric-compactness]]).
91:- *Sequentially compact.* Convergence of a sequence in the metric sense and in
92:  the sense of [[def-sequence-convergence-top]] agree on a metric topology,
93:  because the balls around a point are a neighbourhood base at it
94:  ([[def-metrizable-space]]); the subsequences quantified over are the same.
95:- *Limit point compact.* A point $p$ is a limit point of $A$ in the metric sense
96:  when every ball around $p$ meets $A \setminus \{p\}$, and in the sense above
97:  when every neighbourhood does; the same neighbourhood base makes the two
98:  conditions one ([[def-metrizable-space]],
99:  [[def-interior-closure-boundary-top]]).
100:
101:So no statement below about a metrizable space introduces a second notion, and
102:every theorem of the metric development about these three properties may be
103:quoted here once a metric inducing the topology is named. Lindel&ouml;fness,
104:$\sigma$-compactness and relative compactness have no metric counterpart in this
105:library and are defined here for the first time.
106:
FILE items/def-natural-numbers.md
4:title: "The natural numbers $\\mathbb{N}$ (von Neumann)"
31:## Definition
32:
33:The set of **natural numbers** is the **smallest inductive set**
34:([[def-inductive-set]]),
35:
36:$$\mathbb{N} = \omega := \bigcap\,\{\, I : I \text{ is inductive} \,\},$$
37:
38:which exists and is itself inductive by [[lem-omega-smallest-inductive]] (the
39:Axiom of Infinity supplies one inductive set to intersect within, and Separation
40:makes the intersection a set). On $\mathbb{N}$ we take
41:
42:$$0 := \varnothing, \qquad \sigma(n) := n \cup \{n\},$$
43:
44:the distinguished element and the successor function. Thus
45:$0 = \varnothing$, $1 = \{0\}$, $2 = \{0, 1\}$, $3 = \{0, 1, 2\}$, and in general
46:$n = \{0, 1, \dots, n-1\}$ is the set of its predecessors.
47:
FILE items/def-countable-choice.md
4:title: "The Axiom of Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$)"
42:## Definition
43:
44:The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$, is the following
45:statement.
46:
47:> For every family $(X_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by
48:> $\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\mathbb{N}$ such that
49:> $f(n) \in X_n$ for every $n \in \mathbb{N}$.
50:
51:Equivalently, in the vocabulary of [[def-choice-function]]: every at most
52:countable family of nonempty sets ([[def-countable]]) has a choice function.
53:
FILE items/def-first-uncountable-ordinal.md
4:title: "The first uncountable ordinal $\\omega_1 := \\aleph(\\omega)$"
34:## Definition
35:
36:The **first uncountable ordinal** is
37:
38:$$\omega_1 \;:=\; \aleph(\omega),$$
39:
40:the Hartogs number of $\mathbb{N} = \omega$ ([[thm-hartogs]],
41:[[def-natural-numbers]]): the least ordinal ([[def-ordinal]]) that admits no
42:injective function into $\mathbb{N}$. Equivalently, by that theorem,
43:$\omega_1$ is the set of order types of the well-ordered subsets of
44:$\mathbb{N}$.
45:
46:**Existence is a theorem of ZF.** [[thm-hartogs]] is choice free, so $\omega_1$
47:is available without any choice principle, and its defining property needs
48:none either.
49:
50:**"Uncountable" is [[def-countable]]'s word**, meaning "not at most countable",
51:and it is not redefined here. That $\omega_1$ deserves the name — that it is
52:uncountable, that every ordinal below it is at most countable, that it is a
53:cardinal and a limit ordinal — is proved in
54:[[thm-omega-one-is-the-least-uncountable-ordinal]], which is what discharges the
55:naming obligation of this definition.
56:
FILE items/lem-the-order-topology-on-an-ordinal.md
4:title: "On an ordinal with its order topology the sets $[0,\\beta]$ and $(\\alpha,\\beta]$ form a basis of clopen sets, the isolated points are exactly the non-limit ordinals, and the space is Hausdorff"
38:## Statement
39:
40:Let $\gamma$ be an ordinal ([[def-ordinal]]), regarded as the set of ordinals
41:below it, linearly ordered by membership ([[lem-ordinal-trichotomy]]), and give it
42:the order topology ([[def-order-topology-on-a-linearly-ordered-set]]). For
43:$\alpha, \beta \in \gamma$ write
44:
45:$$[0,\beta] := \{\, \xi \in \gamma : \xi \le \beta \,\}, \qquad (\alpha,\beta] := \{\, \xi \in \gamma : \alpha < \xi \le \beta \,\} .$$
46:
47:Then:
48:
49:1. Every set of either form is **clopen** ([[def-topological-space]]), and
50:   $$\mathcal{B}_\gamma \;:=\; \{\, [0,\beta] : \beta \in \gamma \,\} \;\cup\; \{\, (\alpha,\beta] : \alpha, \beta \in \gamma \,\}$$
51:   is a basis for the order topology of $\gamma$
52:   ([[def-topology-basis-subbasis]]).
53:2. The isolated points of $\gamma$ ([[def-interior-closure-boundary-top]]) are
54:   exactly the ordinals $\xi \in \gamma$ that are $0$ or a successor; a limit
55:   ordinal $\xi \in \gamma$ ([[def-limit-ordinal]]) is not isolated.
56:3. $\gamma$ with its order topology is Hausdorff ([[def-hausdorff-space]]).
57:
58:**Regularity is not claimed here**, and nothing below asserts any separation
59:property beyond claim 3; the finer separation axioms are not available at this
60:point in the reading order.
61:
FILE items/thm-omega-one-is-the-least-uncountable-ordinal.md
4:title: "$\\omega_1$ is uncountable, every ordinal below it is at most countable, it is a cardinal and a limit ordinal, and its existence is a theorem of ZF"
35:## Statement
36:
37:Let $\omega_1 = \aleph(\omega)$ ([[def-first-uncountable-ordinal]]). Then:
38:
39:**(a) The bridge.** An ordinal $\alpha$ ([[def-ordinal]]) injects into
40:$\mathbb{N}$ if and only if $\alpha$ is at most countable
41:([[def-countable]]).
42:
43:**(b)** $\omega_1$ is **uncountable**.
44:
45:**(c)** Every ordinal $\alpha \in \omega_1$ is at most countable; so $\omega_1$
46:is the least uncountable ordinal.
47:
48:**(d)** $\omega_1$ is a **cardinal**, that is an initial ordinal
49:([[def-cardinal]]): no $\alpha \in \omega_1$ is equinumerous with $\omega_1$.
50:
51:**(e)** $\omega_1$ is a **limit ordinal** ([[def-limit-ordinal]]).
52:
53:**All of this is a theorem of ZF and uses no choice principle.** That matters
54:here and is stated deliberately: [[thm-hartogs]] is choice free,
55:[[lem-subset-of-countable]] and [[lem-countable-iff-surjection-from-n]] are
56:choice free, so $\omega_1$ and every property listed above exist in ZF alone.
57:The cost begins two items later on this page, at the boundedness theorem for at
58:most countable subsets of $\omega_1$, which genuinely needs countable choice.
59:
FILE items/def-limit-ordinal.md
4:title: "Successor and limit ordinals"
33:## Definition
34:
35:Let $\alpha$ be an ordinal ([[def-ordinal]]).
36:
37:- $\alpha$ is a **successor ordinal** when $\alpha = \beta^{+} = \beta \cup \{\beta\}$
38:  for some ordinal $\beta$, which is then an ordinal by [[lem-ordinal-basics]];
39:- $\alpha$ is a **limit ordinal** when $\alpha \ne 0$ and $\alpha$ is not a
40:  successor ordinal.
41:
42:Every ordinal is therefore exactly one of: $0$, a successor ordinal, or a limit
43:ordinal. The three cases are the three clauses of a definition or a proof by
44:transfinite recursion or induction over the ordinals.
45:
FILE items/thm-ordinal-spaces-and-compactness.md
4:title: "Every successor ordinal is compact in its order topology and every limit ordinal is not; and, assuming countable choice, $\\omega_1$ is countably compact and sequentially compact while $\\omega_1 + 1$ is compact"
40:## Statement
41:
42:Every ordinal carries the order topology of the membership order on it
43:([[def-ordinal]], [[def-order-topology-on-a-linearly-ordered-set]]), with the
44:clopen basis $\mathcal{B}_\gamma$ of
45:[[lem-the-order-topology-on-an-ordinal]]. Then:
46:
47:1. **Successors are compact.** For every ordinal $\delta$ the successor ordinal
48:   $\delta^{+}$ is compact ([[def-compact-space]]).
49:2. **Limits are not.** No limit ordinal ([[def-limit-ordinal]]) is compact.
50:3. **Assuming the Axiom of Countable Choice** ([[def-countable-choice]]): the
51:   first uncountable ordinal $\omega_1$ ([[def-first-uncountable-ordinal]]) is
52:   sequentially compact and countably compact ([[def-compactness-variants]]),
53:   and it is not compact; while $\omega_1 + 1$ is compact
54:   ([[def-ordinal-addition]]).
55:
56:Claims 1 and 2 are theorems of ZF. Claim 3 spends countable choice twice, both
57:times through cited results that carry the hypothesis in their own statements:
58:[[thm-countable-subsets-of-omega-one-are-bounded]], which supplies the
59:boundedness of at most countable subsets of $\omega_1$, and claim 2 of
60:[[thm-compactness-variants-hierarchy]], which converts sequential compactness
61:into countable compactness; the extraction of a subsequence below selects
62:nothing, taking least elements throughout.
63:

