FILE items/lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space.md
4:title: "The absolute value makes $\\mathbb{R}$ a metric space: $d(x,y) = |x-y|$ is a metric, its open balls are the intervals $(x-r, x+r)$, and it is unbounded"
41:## Statement
42:
43:Define $d_{\mathbb{R}} : \mathbb{R} \times \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ by
44:$d_{\mathbb{R}}(x,y) := |x - y|$ ([[def-abs-value]]). Then:
45:
46:1. $d_{\mathbb{R}}$ is a metric on $\mathbb{R}$ ([[def-metric-space]]); it is
47:   called the **usual metric** of $\mathbb{R}$.
48:2. For $x \in \mathbb{R}$ and $r > 0$ the open ball is the bounded open interval
49:   ([[def-interval]], [[def-metric-ball]])
50:   $$B(x,r) = (x-r,\ x+r),$$
51:   and the closed ball is $\bar B(x,r) = [x-r,\ x+r]$.
52:3. Consequently $U \subseteq \mathbb{R}$ is open in the metric topology of
53:   $d_{\mathbb{R}}$ ([[def-metric-topology]]) exactly when for every $x \in U$
54:   there is $r > 0$ with $(x-r, x+r) \subseteq U$. This topology is called the
55:   **usual topology** of $\mathbb{R}$.
56:4. $(\mathbb{R}, d_{\mathbb{R}})$ is not a bounded metric space
57:   ([[def-metric-bounded-diameter]]): no ball contains $\mathbb{R}$, so
58:   $\operatorname{diam}(\mathbb{R})$ is not defined.
59:
FILE items/thm-heine-borel-rn.md
4:title: "Heine-Borel in $\\mathbb{R}^n$: with the Euclidean metric a subset of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is compact if and only if it is closed and bounded, and the proof by bisection uses no choice principle; the same holds on the real line"
35:## Statement
36:
37:Let $n \in \mathbb{N}$ with $n \ge 1$, let $\mathbb{R}^n$ be the set of functions
38:$n \to \mathbb{R}$ and let $d_2$ be the Euclidean metric on it
39:([[lem-metrics-on-rn]]). Then:
40:
41:1. **Closed boxes are compact.** For reals $a_k \le b_k$ $(k < n)$ the box
42:   $Q = \{\, x \in \mathbb{R}^n : a_k \le x_k \le b_k \text{ for every } k < n \,\}$
43:   is a compact subset of $(\mathbb{R}^n, d_2)$ ([[def-metric-compactness]]).
44:2. **Heine-Borel.** A subset $K \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n$ is a compact subset of
45:   $(\mathbb{R}^n, d_2)$ if and only if $K$ is closed in $\mathbb{R}^n$
46:   ([[def-metric-topology]]) and bounded
47:   ([[def-metric-bounded-diameter]]).
48:3. **The real line.** A subset $K \subseteq \mathbb{R}$ is a compact subset of
49:   $(\mathbb{R}, d_{\mathbb{R}})$, the usual metric $d_{\mathbb{R}}(x,y) = |x-y|$
50:   ([[lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space]]), if and only if $K$ is closed in
51:   $\mathbb{R}$ and bounded.
52:
53:**No choice principle is used.** The bisection below halves one coordinate at a
54:time and takes the **left** half whenever the left half still fails to be finitely
55:covered, the right half otherwise: a rule with two outcomes, decided by a
56:property of the box, not a selection. That is the whole reason the theorem is
57:available in ZF, while the general "complete and totally bounded implies compact"
58:([[thm-complete-and-totally-bounded-implies-compact]]) is not.
59:
60:The hypothesis $n \ge 1$ is inherited from [[lem-metrics-on-rn]], which defines
61:$\mathbb{R}^n$ and its metrics only there; the last remark below records what
62:happens at $n = 0$.
63:
FILE items/thm-compactness-agrees-with-metric-compactness.md
4:title: "For a metric space with its metric topology, compactness in the topological sense is compactness in the metric sense, and the two notions of compact subset coincide"
41:## Statement
42:
43:Let $(X,d)$ be a metric space ([[def-metric-space]]) and let $\mathcal{T}_d$ be
44:its metric topology ([[def-metric-topology]]), so that $(X, \mathcal{T}_d)$ is a
45:topological space ([[def-topological-space]]) and is metrizable
46:([[def-metrizable-space]]). Then:
47:
48:1. $(X,d)$ is a compact metric space ([[def-metric-compactness]]) **if and only
49:   if** $(X, \mathcal{T}_d)$ is a compact topological space
50:   ([[def-compact-space]]).
51:2. For every $A \subseteq X$: $A$ is a compact subset of the metric space
52:   $(X,d)$ if and only if $A$ is a compact subset of the topological space
53:   $(X, \mathcal{T}_d)$, the two readings of "compact subset" being the metric
54:   subspace $(A, d_A)$ ([[def-isometry-and-metric-embedding]]) and the topological
55:   subspace $(A, (\mathcal{T}_d)_A)$ ([[def-subspace-topology-top]]).
56:
57:**Nothing here is a coincidence and nothing is transported.** The open-cover
58:condition of [[def-metric-compactness]] quantifies over families of subsets open
59:in $(X,d)$, and by [[def-metric-topology]] those are exactly the members of
60:$\mathcal{T}_d$; so the two conditions are not merely equivalent, they are the
61:same condition written twice. No choice principle is used.
62:
FILE items/lem-of-q-dense.md
4:title: "ℚ is dense in every Archimedean ordered field"
30:## Statement
31:
32:Let $F$ be an Archimedean ordered field ([[def-archimedean-field]]) and let
33:$\iota : \mathbb{Q} \to F$ be the canonical embedding ([[lem-of-q-embeds]]). Then
34:$\iota(\mathbb{Q})$ is dense in $F$: for any $x < y$ in $F$ there is a rational
35:$q$ with $x < \iota(q) < y$.
36:
FILE items/lem-q-and-irrationals-dense-r.md
4:title: "Both $\\mathbb{Q}$ and $\\mathbb{R} \\setminus \\mathbb{Q}$ are dense in $\\mathbb{R}$, and every nonempty open subset of $\\mathbb{R}$ is uncountable"
35:## Statement
36:
37:Write $\mathbb{Q}_{\mathbb{R}}$ for the image of $\mathbb{Q}$ in $\mathbb{R}$
38:under the canonical embedding $q \mapsto \hat q$ ([[lem-rat-embeds-dense]]), the
39:set usually written $\mathbb{Q}$ once the identification is made, and put
40:$X := \mathbb{R} \setminus \mathbb{Q}_{\mathbb{R}}$ for the irrationals. Then:
41:
42:1. $\mathbb{Q}_{\mathbb{R}}$ is dense in $\mathbb{R}$, that is,
43:   $\overline{\mathbb{Q}_{\mathbb{R}}} = \mathbb{R}$ ([[def-limit-point-r]]);
44:2. $X$ is dense in $\mathbb{R}$;
45:3. every nonempty open subset of $\mathbb{R}$ is uncountable
46:   ([[def-countable]]).
47:
48:Claim 2 is not a symmetry of claim 1: the rationals are dense because they are
49:*constructed* to approximate, whereas the irrationals are dense because there are
50:too many points in any interval for a countable set to exhaust it, which is why
51:claim 3 is proved alongside and used for it.
52:
FILE items/thm-closure-characterisation-top.md
4:title: "A point lies in the closure of $A$ iff every basic neighbourhood of it meets $A$; the closure is the smallest closed superset and equals $A$ together with its derived set"
35:## Statement
36:
37:Let $(X, \mathcal{T})$ be a topological space, let $\mathcal{B}$ be a basis for
38:$\mathcal{T}$ ([[def-topology-basis-subbasis]]), let $A \subseteq X$ and let
39:$x \in X$. Closure, derived set and limit points are as in
40:[[def-interior-closure-boundary-top]]. Then:
41:
42:1. The following four conditions are equivalent.
43:   - (a) $x \in \overline{A}$;
44:   - (b) $N \cap A \ne \varnothing$ for every neighbourhood $N$ of $x$
45:     ([[def-neighbourhood-top]]);
46:   - (c) $U \cap A \ne \varnothing$ for every open $U$ with $x \in U$;
47:   - (d) $B \cap A \ne \varnothing$ for every $B \in \mathcal{B}$ with $x \in B$.
48:2. $\overline{A}$ is closed, contains $A$, and is contained in every closed
49:   $F \subseteq X$ with $A \subseteq F$; so it is the smallest closed superset of
50:   $A$, and $A$ is closed if and only if $A = \overline{A}$.
51:3. $\overline{A} = A \cup A'$.
52:
53:Claim 2 is recorded here for reference and is discharged in
54:[[def-interior-closure-boundary-top]], where it is what makes the definition of
55:$\overline{A}$ well posed; claims 1 and 3 are proved below. Claim 1 is the form in
56:which the closure is used everywhere afterwards, and clause (d) is what makes a
57:closure computable from a basis rather than from all open sets.
58:
FILE items/thm-locally-compact-hausdorff-basics.md
4:title: "In a locally compact Hausdorff space every point has a neighbourhood base of compact sets, every open subspace and every closed subspace is locally compact, every open set around a point contains an open set with compact closure inside it, and every compact set sits inside an open set with compact closure"
42:## Statement
43:
44:Let $(X, \mathcal{T})$ be a topological space ([[def-topological-space]]). Then:
45:
46:1. **A neighbourhood base of compact sets.** If $X$ is locally compact
47:   ([[def-locally-compact-space]]) and Hausdorff ([[def-hausdorff-space]]), then
48:   every neighbourhood $N$ of a point $x \in X$ ([[def-neighbourhood-top]])
49:   contains a compact neighbourhood of $x$; so the compact neighbourhoods of $x$
50:   form a neighbourhood base at $x$.
51:2. **Heredity along open and closed subspaces.** If $X$ is locally compact and
52:   Hausdorff and $S \subseteq X$ is open, then the subspace $S$ is locally
53:   compact ([[def-subspace-topology-top]]). If $X$ is locally compact and
54:   $F \subseteq X$ is closed, then the subspace $F$ is locally compact; no
55:   Hausdorff hypothesis is used for this half.
56:3. **Shrinking inside an open set.** If $X$ is locally compact and Hausdorff,
57:   $O \subseteq X$ is open and $x \in O$, there is an open $V$ with
58:   $$x \in V \subseteq \overline{V} \subseteq O$$
59:   and $\overline{V}$ a compact subset of $X$.
60:4. **Compact sets sit in open sets with compact closure.** If $X$ is locally
61:   compact and Hausdorff and $K \subseteq X$ is compact, there is an open
62:   $V \subseteq X$ with $K \subseteq V$ and $\overline{V}$ a compact subset of
63:   $X$ ([[def-interior-closure-boundary-top]]).
64:
65:**No choice principle is used**; every cover produced below is defined by a
66:formula and thinned by [[lem-compactness-of-a-subspace-is-ambient]], which returns
67:members rather than indices.
68:

