FILE items/thm-the-long-line-is-a-connected-linear-continuum.md
4:title: "The long ray is a linear continuum, hence connected; every one of its at most countable subsets is bounded above, assuming countable choice"
46:## Statement
47:
48:Let $R = \omega_1 \times [0,1)$ be the closed long ray with its lexicographic
49:order and its order topology ([[def-the-long-line]]). Then:
50:
51:1. **$R$ is a linear continuum**
52:   ([[def-order-topology-on-a-linearly-ordered-set]]): it has at least two
53:   elements, it is order-dense, and it has the least upper bound property.
54:2. **$R$ is connected**, and so is every order-convex subset of $R$; in
55:   particular every initial segment
56:   $[0_R, x] = \{\, y \in R : y \le x \,\}$ is connected.
57:3. **Assuming the Axiom of Countable Choice $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$**
58:   ([[def-countable-choice]]): every at most countable subset of $R$
59:   ([[def-countable]]) has an upper bound in $R$; so no at most countable subset
60:   of $R$ is unbounded above.
61:
62:Claims 1 and 2 are theorems of ZF. Claim 3 carries the hypothesis because it is
63:inherited whole from [[thm-countable-subsets-of-omega-one-are-bounded]], whose
64:own statement carries it, and it is spent at exactly one step below.
65:
66:**Nothing here says $R$ is path-connected**, and this proof gives no path
67:between two of its points; that question needs an order isomorphism of each
68:initial segment with $[0,1]$, which is not constructed on this page.
69:

